  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday morning through next Sunday)\nIssued at 230 PM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nDry and Warm Weather Through Mid-Week:\n\nThe expansive mid-level ridge will slide east on Wednesday, \ncentering over the central and southern Appalachians. For North and \nCentral GA, this will result in another warm and mostly rain-free \nday. Tropical moisture from over the Atlantic/Florida will start to \npush northward into the southern side of the ridge, so we can't rule \nout some afternoon showers and storms in far southeast areas. High \ntemps will remain in the low to mid 90s, with heat index values \npeaking around 100-105.\n\nRain/Thunderstorm Potential Returns Late Week into the Weekend:\n\nUncertainty in the forecast jumps up substantially for the Thu/Fri \ntime period, as models have significant spread in how the synoptic \npattern evolves. Over the past 24 hours, there has been an uptick in \nthe number of ensemble members that have a shortwave trough and slug \nof tropical moisture surging up across Florida into Georgia and \nsqueezing out the mid-level ridge. With PWAT values of 2-2.5\", this \nwould support numerous to widespread showers and storms each day, \nbecoming isolated to scattered over the weekend as moisture \ndecreases. On the flip side, there is still a group of ensemble \nmembers that have the ridge holding in place and shunting the \ntropical moisture off to the southwest, which would keep rain \nchances very low over our area. With either solution (and anything \nin between), temperatures will remain at or above normal through the \nweekend, with highs in the upper 80s to low 90s. Confidence in the \nlate week / weekend forecast will continue to grow over the next 24-\n48 hours, so stay tuned for updates!",
